Let $A = \begin{pmatrix} \sec^2 x & 1 & 1 \\ \cos^2 x & \cos^2 x & \csc^2 x \\ 1 & \cos^2 x & \cot^2 x \end{pmatrix}$. We want to find the determinant of $A$.
\begin{align*} \label{eq:1} \det(A) &= \begin{vmatrix} \sec^2 x & 1 & 1 \\ \cos^2 x & \cos^2 x & \csc^2 x \\ 1 & \cos^2 x & \cot^2 x \end{vmatrix} \\ &= \sec^2 x \begin{vmatrix} \cos^2 x & \csc^2 x \\ \cos^2 x & \cot^2 x \end{vmatrix} - 1 \begin{vmatrix} \cos^2 x & \csc^2 x \\ 1 & \cot^2 x \end{vmatrix} + 1 \begin{vmatrix} \cos^2 x & \cos^2 x \\ 1 & \cos^2 x \end{vmatrix} \\ &= \sec^2 x (\cos^2 x \cot^2 x - \csc^2 x \cos^2 x) - (\cos^2 x \cot^2 x - \csc^2 x) + (\cos^4 x - \cos^2 x) \\ &= \sec^2 x (\cos^2 x \frac{\cos^2 x}{\sin^2 x} - \csc^2 x \cos^2 x) - \frac{\cos^2 x \cos^2 x}{\sin^2 x} + \csc^2 x + \cos^4 x - \cos^2 x \\ &= \sec^2 x (\frac{\cos^4 x}{\sin^2 x} - \frac{\cos^2 x}{\sin^2 x}) - \frac{\cos^4 x}{\sin^2 x} + \csc^2 x + \cos^4 x - \cos^2 x \\ &= \frac{\sec^2 x \cos^4 x}{\sin^2 x} - \frac{\sec^2 x \cos^2 x}{\sin^2 x} - \frac{\cos^4 x}{\sin^2 x} + \csc^2 x + \cos^4 x - \cos^2 x\end{aligned}
However, this seems to get messy, so let's try a different approach. 
We expand along the first row:
\begin{align*} \det(A) &= \sec^2 x \begin{vmatrix} \cos^2 x & \csc^2 x \\ \cos^2 x & \cot^2 x \end{vmatrix} - 1 \begin{vmatrix} \cos^2 x & \csc^2 x \\ 1 & \cot^2 x \end{vmatrix} + 1 \begin{vmatrix} \cos^2 x & \cos^2 x \\ 1 & \cos^2 x \end{vmatrix} \\ &= \sec^2 x (\cos^2 x \cot^2 x - \csc^2 x \cos^2 x) - (\cos^2 x \cot^2 x - \csc^2 x) + (\cos^4 x - \cos^2 x) \\ &= \sec^2 x (\cos^2 x \frac{\cos^2 x}{\sin^2 x} - \frac{1}{\sin^2 x} \cos^2 x) - \frac{\cos^2 x \cos^2 x}{\sin^2 x} + \csc^2 x + \cos^4 x - \cos^2 x \\ &= \sec^2 x \left( \frac{\cos^4 x}{\sin^2 x} - \frac{\cos^2 x}{\sin^2 x} \right) - \frac{\cos^4 x}{\sin^2 x} + \csc^2 x + \cos^4 x - \cos^2 x \\ &= \frac{\sec^2 x \cos^4 x}{\sin^2 x} - \frac{\sec^2 x \cos^2 x}{\sin^2 x} - \frac{\cos^4 x}{\sin^2 x} + \csc^2 x + \cos^4 x - \cos^2 x \end{align*}
Let's try another approach. Since $\sec^2 x = 1 + \tan^2 x$, $\csc^2 x = 1 + \cot^2 x$, $\cot^2 x = \csc^2 x - 1$,
\begin{align*} \det(A) &= \begin{vmatrix} \sec^2 x & 1 & 1 \\ \cos^2 x & \cos^2 x & \csc^2 x \\ 1 & \cos^2 x & \cot^2 x \end{vmatrix} \\ &= \begin{vmatrix} 1 + \tan^2 x & 1 & 1 \\ \cos^2 x & \cos^2 x & 1+\cot^2 x \\ 1 & \cos^2 x & \cot^2 x \end{vmatrix} \end{align*}

When $x = \frac{\pi}{4}$, $\sec^2 x = 2$, $\cos^2 x = \frac{1}{2}$, $\csc^2 x = 2$, $\cot^2 x = 1$.
\[A = \begin{pmatrix} 2 & 1 & 1 \\ \frac{1}{2} & \frac{1}{2} & 2 \\ 1 & \frac{1}{2} & 1 \end{pmatrix} \]
Then
\begin{align*} \det(A) &= 2 \begin{vmatrix} \frac{1}{2} & 2 \\ \frac{1}{2} & 1 \end{vmatrix} - 1 \begin{vmatrix} \frac{1}{2} & 2 \\ 1 & 1 \end{vmatrix} + 1 \begin{vmatrix} \frac{1}{2} & \frac{1}{2} \\ 1 & \frac{1}{2} \end{vmatrix} \\ &= 2 \left( \frac{1}{2} - 1 \right) - 1 \left( \frac{1}{2} - 2 \right) + 1 \left( \frac{1}{4} - \frac{1}{2} \right) \\ &= 2 \left( -\frac{1}{2} \right) - 1 \left( -\frac{3}{2} \right) + 1 \left( -\frac{1}{4} \right) \\ &= -1 + \frac{3}{2} - \frac{1}{4} = \frac{-4 + 6 - 1}{4} = \frac{1}{4} \end{align*} 

