Let $A = \begin{pmatrix} \sec^2 x & 1 & 1 \\ \cos^2 x & \cos^2 x & \csc^2 x \\ 1 & \cos^2 x & \cot^2 x \end{pmatrix}$. We want to find $\det(A)$. We have
\begin{align*} \det(A) &= \sec^2 x \begin{vmatrix} \cos^2 x & \csc^2 x \\ \cos^2 x & \cot^2 x \end{vmatrix} - 1 \begin{vmatrix} \cos^2 x & \csc^2 x \\ 1 & \cot^2 x \end{vmatrix} + 1 \begin{vmatrix} \cos^2 x & \cos^2 x \\ 1 & \cos^2 x \end{vmatrix} \\ &= \sec^2 x (\cos^2 x \cot^2 x - \csc^2 x \cos^2 x) - (\cos^2 x \cot^2 x - \csc^2 x) + (\cos^4 x - \cos^2 x) \\ &= \sec^2 x (\cos^2 x \frac{\cos^2 x}{\sin^2 x} - \frac{1}{\sin^2 x} \cos^2 x) - \cos^2 x \frac{\cos^2 x}{\sin^2 x} + \frac{1}{\sin^2 x} + \cos^4 x - \cos^2 x \\ &= \sec^2 x \left( \frac{\cos^4 x}{\sin^2 x} - \frac{\cos^2 x}{\sin^2 x} \right) - \frac{\cos^4 x}{\sin^2 x} + \frac{1}{\sin^2 x} + \cos^4 x - \cos^2 x \\ &= \frac{\sec^2 x}{\sin^2 x} (\cos^4 x - \cos^2 x) - \frac{\cos^4 x}{\sin^2 x} + \frac{1}{\sin^2 x} + \cos^4 x - \cos^2 x \\ &= \frac{1}{\sin^2 x} (\frac{1}{\cos^2 x} \cos^4 x - \frac{1}{\cos^2 x} \cos^2 x) - \frac{\cos^4 x}{\sin^2 x} + \frac{1}{\sin^2 x} + \cos^4 x - \cos^2 x \\ &= \frac{1}{\sin^2 x} (\cos^2 x - 1) - \frac{\cos^4 x}{\sin^2 x} + \frac{1}{\sin^2 x} + \cos^4 x - \cos^2 x \\ &= \frac{\cos^2 x - 1}{\sin^2 x} - \frac{\cos^4 x}{\sin^2 x} + \frac{1}{\sin^2 x} + \cos^4 x - \cos^2 x \\ &= \frac{\cos^2 x - 1}{\sin^2 x} - \frac{\cos^4 x}{\sin^2 x} + \frac{1}{\sin^2 x} + \cos^4 x - \cos^2 x \\ &= \frac{\cos^2 x - 1}{\sin^2 x} - \frac{\cos^4 x}{\sin^2 x} + \frac{1}{\sin^2 x} + \cos^4 x - \cos^2 x\end{aligned} 
However, there is a simpler approach. 
Let $A = \begin{pmatrix} \sec^2 x & 1 & 1 \\ \cos^2 x & \cos^2 x & \csc^2 x \\ 1 & \cos^2 x & \cot^2 x \end{pmatrix}$. 
The determinant is
\begin{align*} \label{eq:1} \det(A) &= \sec^2 x \left( \cos^2 x \cot^2 x - \csc^2 x \cos^2 x \right) - 1 \left( \cos^2 x \cot^2 x - \csc^2 x \right) + 1 \left( \cos^4 x - \cos^2 x \right) \\ &= \sec^2 x \left( \cos^2 x \frac{\cos^2 x}{\sin^2 x} - \frac{1}{\sin^2 x} \cos^2 x \right) - \left( \cos^2 x \frac{\cos^2 x}{\sin^2 x} - \frac{1}{\sin^2 x} \right) + \cos^4 x - \cos^2 x \\ &= \sec^2 x \left( \frac{\cos^4 x}{\sin^2 x} - \frac{\cos^2 x}{\sin^2 x} \right) - \frac{\cos^4 x}{\sin^2 x} + \frac{1}{\sin^2 x} + \cos^4 x - \cos^2 x \\ &= \frac{\sec^2 x}{\sin^2 x} (\cos^4 x - \cos^2 x) - \frac{\cos^4 x}{\sin^2 x} + \frac{1}{\sin^2 x} + \cos^4 x - \cos^2 x \end{align*} 
Let $x = \frac{\pi}{4}$. Then $\sec^2 \frac{\pi}{4} = 2$, $\cos^2 \frac{\pi}{4} = \frac{1}{2}$, $\csc^2 \frac{\pi}{4} = 2$, $\cot^2 \frac{\pi}{4} = 1$. 
