Let $n$ be the number of members in the math club. We are given that $n = 20$.
We need to select a president, a vice-president, and a treasurer such that no member can hold more than one office.
This is a permutation problem since the order in which we select the members matters (president, vice-president, treasurer are different roles).
The number of ways to select a president, a vice-president, and a treasurer from the 20 members is the number of permutations of 20 members taken 3 at a time, which is denoted as $P(20, 3)$ or $_{20}P_3$.

The formula for permutations is $P(n, r) = \frac{n!}{(n-r)!}$, where $n$ is the total number of items and $r$ is the number of items we are selecting and arranging.
In our case, $n = 20$ and $r = 3$.
So, $P(20, 3) = \frac{20!}{(20-3)!} = \frac{20!}{17!} = 20 \times 19 \times 18$.

Calculating the product:
$20 \times 19 = 380$
$380 \times 18 = 380 \times (10 + 8) = 3800 + 380 \times 8 = 3800 + 3040 = 6840$

Alternatively,
$20 \times 19 \times 18 = 20 \times (19 \times 18) = 20 \times (19 \times (20 - 2)) = 20 \times (380 - 38) = 20 \times 342 = 6840$

Another way:
$20 \times 19 \times 18 = (20 \times 18) \times 19 = (360) \times 19 = 360 \times (20 - 1) = 7200 - 360 = 6840$.

Thus, the number of ways to select a president, a vice-president, and a treasurer is 6840.

