The volume of a cube is equal to the side length cubed.
\boxed{8} \text{ cubic centimeters}
The original volume of the cube is $2 \times 2 \times 2 = 8$ cubic centimeters.
The increase in volume is 19 cubic centimeters.
The new volume is $8 + 19 = 27$ cubic centimeters.
Let $s$ be the side length of the new cube. Then $s^3 = 27$.
Taking the cube root of both sides, we get $s = \sqrt[3]{27} = 3$.
The length of one edge of the new cube is 3 centimeters.
